 :the-structural-finding
 {:statement "Any PER-BLOCK auxiliary tier pays 84x on this path and cannot be
              amortised, because the reads are sequential by construction and
              there is no batch to amortise over."
  :already-known-from-the-other-side
  "iteration-04 recorded that h4 (CARv2 packing) is worth only $4.32 here
   because each novelty cell is its own commit and therefore its own pack. That
   is the same fact: a chain the reader walks one link at a time defeats
   coalescing whether the coalescing is done by a pack or by a cache."
  :what-it-implies
  "h1-seed-materialisation is the version of this idea the path can use: ONE
   object keyed by the chain CID, one read, one write. h8 was h1 applied per
   block. This makes h1 MORE interesting than its rank suggests, not less."}

 :what-the-tests-could-not-catch
 "131 tests, 828 assertions, and a break/restore that produced exactly 2
  failures in the new test with 0 collateral -- the discipline this workspace
  asks for, followed, and it did not catch this. Every test ran under Node with
  a FAKE `caches` whose match/put are in-memory map operations. The suite proved
  the read path CONSULTS L2 and attributes the answer to it, which is what it
  claimed. It could not price a real Cache API call, and nothing had a cost
  floor that would notice 168 of them.

  A test that a tier is REACHED is not a test that the tier is AFFORDABLE, and
  affordability was the whole hypothesis."

 :what-the-judge-could-not-catch
 "`cost-per-million` prices CPU, class-B and requests. It has no term for `the
  request does not complete`, so h8 scores $0.00 -- identical to a hypothesis
  that changes nothing -- and it has no way to see 168 added Cache API calls,
  which are neither CPU it models nor class-B it counts. Both are now recorded
  in :what-the-judge-cannot-see. **A ranking is an ordering of candidates and
  must never be the only gate before a deploy.**"

